md`# Cloudflare from a notebook

A service declared as a notebook value. \`cloudflare.Service(name, fn, options)\` returns a service: a name, a function that answers requests, and a manifest (methods, paths, rules, \`calls\`). Call it in the tab with \`service.fetch(request)\`, read the module it would upload with \`service.emit()\`, run that module in the tab with \`simulate(service)\`. \`cloudflare.Worker\` is the name of \`Service\` until 2026-10-07 and is the same function.

A service is deployed as one Cloudflare Worker, and it is not a bare Worker. Its code runs inside a wrapper (\`workerRuntime\`) and reaches the Brain only through platform cells: \`secrets\`, \`sql\`, \`inbox\` and \`xrpc\` are calls to the core, which decides each one by a rule; \`rows\` are the service's own rows; \`blobs\` are its own keys in one bucket. The deployer builds the Worker's bindings from the platform cells the code reaches, so a service cannot name a binding. \`secrets.NAME\` answers a name in the manifest's \`secrets\` and no other: a database, a bucket or a Worker bound under that name reads as unset, as does any name that starts \`BRAIN_\`. A Hono service is handed \`{ BRAIN_INFO }\` as its \`env\`, not the Worker's bindings. A service holds no Cloudflare token unless it declares the permission groups it needs (\`cloudflare: ["Workers Observability Read"]\`): the deployer then holds the deploy for the owner, mints a token of exactly those groups and binds it to that Worker alone, and the service sends it with the \`cloudflareApi\` cell. \`log\` writes one line to Workers Logs with the Worker's name and the ray of the call. Its Worker does not answer the internet: a request comes through the kernel and the core. In mode \`enforce\` the core refuses a call to a method the service does not list in \`calls\`. Outbound \`fetch\` is not restricted. \`browser\` is Cloudflare Browser Run (remote browsers, billed by the browser-hour): the approval page names a service that reaches it, and a member's service is refused it.

The module is the function plus every cell it depends on, across imports, written out in dependency order with no Observable runtime. The walk stops at **platform cells** (\`secrets\`, \`rows\`, \`sql\`, \`inbox\`, \`xrpc\`), which work in the notebook and are replaced on the Worker by the Cloudflare resource they stand for, and at **library cells** (\`library(name, file)\`), which are uploaded as a second file.

Refused, naming the cell: a generator, a \`viewof\` or \`mutable\` cell, a cell that depends on a notebook builtin such as \`html\` or \`width\`, and a secret read by a computed name.

\`service.deploy(reason, was)\` needs a Brain, a reason, which the deployer logs, and the hash you saw running (\`null\` for no Worker): with none configured it throws. \`serviceList\` shows each declared service against what the deployer runs. There is no \`service.status\`.`
// %%
md`## Services in this notebook

A module announces a service with \`plugins.add("workers", service, { invalidation })\`, in a cell of its own. \`declaredWorkers\` is every service announced on the page, and \`serviceList\` shows them against what a deployer says is running, with one Apply.

A service's hash is recomputed when its cell or any cell it reaches changes, because that re-runs the cell that announces it.`
// %%
declaredWorkers = plugins.get("workers")
// %%
// One row per declared service: what the tab would deploy against what the deployer deployed and the Worker reports.
// Then one row per Worker the deployer runs that this notebook does not declare: its source is somewhere else, or lost.
serviceRows = (emitted, deployerState, reported = {}, registry = {}) => [
  ...emitted.map((e) => {
    if (e.error) return { name: e.name, worker: null, state: "cannot deploy", note: e.error };
    const worker = e.meta.worker;
    const rec = deployerState ? (deployerState.workers || []).find((w) => w.worker === worker) : null;
    const running = rec && rec.hash ? rec.hash : null;
    const k = deployerState && deployerState.kernel ? deployerState.kernel[worker] : null;
    const waiting = deployerState ? (deployerState.pending || []).some((p) => p.hash === e.hash) : false;
    const approved = deployerState ? (deployerState.approved || []).includes(e.hash) : false;
    // asDeployed: the page loaded this module from the Worker and it has not been edited since.
    const mine = e.asDeployed || e.hash;
    const route = registry[worker];
    const state = !deployerState ? "unknown" : !running ? "not installed" : reported[worker] && reported[worker] !== running ? "skewed" : rec.fails >= 2 ? "failing" : running === mine ? "in sync" : "changed";
    const note = waiting ? "waiting for approval"
      : approved && state !== "in sync" ? "approved, press Apply"
      : rec && rec.lastError && rec.failedHash === e.hash ? `put back: ${rec.lastError}`
      : k && k.state === "probation" ? "on probation"
      : route && route.hash === running && route.verified && !route.verified.ok ? `its tests failed: ${(route.verified.failed || []).join(", ") || route.verified.reason}`
      : route && route.hash === running && route.failing ? `verified, and its tests fail now: ${(route.failing.failed || []).join(", ") || route.failing.reason}`
      : route && route.hash === running && !route.verified ? "unverified: its tests have not been run from this version"
      : state === "skewed" ? `${worker} is running ${String(reported[worker]).slice(0, 12)}, which the deployer did not deploy.`
      : "";
    return { name: e.name, worker, role: e.meta.role, module: e.meta.module || null, methods: e.meta.methods, calls: e.meta.calls || [], paths: e.meta.paths.map((p) => p.path), secrets: e.meta.secrets, hash: e.hash, running, state, verified: route && route.hash === running ? !!(route.verified && route.verified.ok) : null, note };
  }),
  ...((deployerState && deployerState.workers) || [])
    .filter((w) => w.hash && !emitted.some((e) => e.meta && e.meta.worker === w.worker))
    .map((w) => ({ name: w.worker, worker: w.worker, role: null, methods: [], paths: [], secrets: [], hash: null, running: w.hash, state: "no source", note: `${w.worker} is running and this notebook does not declare it. Its source is in another copy of the notebook, or was not saved.` }))
]
// %%
// The Services table. \`brain\` is { state(), info(worker), apply(emitted), deployerUrl }; with none it lists what is declared.
// \`moduleLink(module, label)\` gives the node shown for a Worker's name, a link to its module; with none the name is plain.
serviceList = ({ services = [], brain = null, moduleLink = null } = {}) => {
  const hue = { "in sync": "#2e9e5b", "not installed": "#2e9e5b", changed: "#e6a700", unknown: "var(--theme-foreground-muted)", skewed: "#e5484d", failing: "#e5484d", "cannot deploy": "#e5484d", "no source": "#e5484d" };
  const body = htl.html`<div></div>`;
  const root = htl.html`<div style="font:14px/1.45 var(--sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif);color:var(--theme-foreground)">${body}</div>`;
  let emitted = [], deployerState = null, reported = {}, registry = {}, busy = "", results = [], reason = "";
  const short = (h) => (h ? String(h).slice(0, 12) : "none");
  const draw = () => {
    const rows = serviceRows(emitted, deployerState, reported, registry);
    const differing = rows.filter((r) => ["changed", "not installed"].includes(r.state));
    root.value = rows;
    const red = (note, state) => /put back|cannot|did not deploy|tests failed|tests fail now/.test(note) || state === "cannot deploy";
    const table = Inputs.table(
      rows.map((r) => ({ worker: r.worker || r.name, module: r.module || null, serves: [...(r.methods || []).map((m) => m.replace("com.lopecode.brain.", "")), ...(r.paths || [])].join(", "), version: r.hash, state: r.state, tests: root.tests[r.worker] || "", note: r.note || "", running: r.running })),
      {
        columns: ["worker", "serves", "version", "state", "tests", "note"],
        select: false,
        layout: "auto",
        rows: 40,
        format: {
          worker: (v, i, data) => htl.html`<b>${(moduleLink && data[i].module && moduleLink(data[i].module, v)) || v}</b>`,
          version: (v, i, data) => htl.html`<code>${short(v)}</code>${data[i].running && data[i].running !== v ? htl.html` <span style="color:var(--theme-foreground-muted)">running <code>${short(data[i].running)}</code></span>` : ""}`,
          state: (v) => htl.html`<span style=${`color:${hue[v]}`}>${v}</span>`,
          note: (v, i, data) => htl.html`<span style=${`white-space:normal;color:${red(v, data[i].state) ? "#e5484d" : "var(--theme-foreground-muted)"}`}>${v}</span>`
        }
      }
    );
    // The deployer refuses a change with no reason, and logs the one it is given.
    const why = htl.html`<input placeholder="Reason for the change" maxlength="300" style="font:inherit;min-width:18em;flex:1">`;
    why.value = reason;
    why.oninput = () => (reason = why.value);
    const apply = Inputs.button(`Apply ${differing.length} change${differing.length === 1 ? "" : "s"}`, { disabled: !brain || !differing.length || !!busy, reduce: () => void run(differing) });
    const again = Inputs.button("Refresh", { disabled: !brain || !!busy, reduce: () => void refresh() });
    const waiting = results.filter((r) => r.state === "waiting");
    const refused = results.filter((r) => r.state === "refused" || r.state === "put-back");
    body.replaceChildren(htl.html`${table}
      <div style="margin-top:8px;display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">${why}${apply}${again}<span style="color:var(--theme-foreground-muted)">${busy}</span></div>
      ${waiting.length ? htl.html`<div style="margin-top:6px;color:var(--theme-foreground-muted)">Waiting for approval: ${waiting.map((r) => r.worker).join(", ")}. ${brain && brain.deployerUrl ? htl.html`Approve on the <a href=${brain.deployerUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">deployer page</a>, then press Apply again.` : ""}</div>` : ""}
      ${refused.map((r) => htl.html`<div style="margin-top:6px;color:#e5484d">${r.state === "refused" ? "Refused: " + r.reason : `${r.worker} failed its health check and was put back to ${short(r.previous)}. ${r.reason}`}</div>`)}`);
  };
  const refresh = async () => {
    emitted = await Promise.all(services.map((s) => s.emit().catch((e) => ({ name: s.name, error: String((e && e.message) || e) }))));
    emitted.sort((a, b) => String(a.meta ? a.meta.worker : a.name).localeCompare(String(b.meta ? b.meta.worker : b.name)));
    if (brain && brain.asDeployed) for (const e of emitted) if (e.meta) e.asDeployed = brain.asDeployed(e);
    draw();
    if (!brain) return;
    busy = "reading what is running…";
    draw();
    try {
      deployerState = await brain.state();
      const infos = await Promise.all(emitted.filter((e) => e.meta).map((e) => brain.info(e.meta.worker).catch(() => null)));
      reported = Object.fromEntries(infos.filter(Boolean).map((i) => [i.name, i.hash]));
      registry = brain.registry ? Object.fromEntries((await brain.registry().catch(() => [])).map((r) => [r.worker, r])) : {};
      busy = "";
    } catch (e) {
      busy = "could not read the deployer: " + String((e && e.message) || e);
    }
    draw();
    root.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("input", { bubbles: true }));
  };
  const run = async (rows) => {
    results = [];
    // The core before anything bound to it, the kernel last.
    const order = (r) => (r.role === "core" ? 0 : r.role === "kernel" ? 2 : 1);
    for (const r of [...rows].sort((a, b) => order(a) - order(b))) {
      busy = `applying ${r.worker}…`;
      draw();
      try {
        // r.running is what this page last read from the deployer: the view the owner pressed Apply on.
        const out = await brain.apply(emitted.find((e) => e.meta && e.meta.worker === r.worker), { reason, was: r.running });
        results.push(...out.results);
      } catch (e) {
        results.push({ worker: r.worker, state: "refused", reason: String((e && e.message) || e) });
      }
    }
    busy = "";
    await refresh();
  };
  root.refresh = refresh;
  // worker -> text for the tests column, set by whoever ran them.
  root.tests = {};
  root.value = [];
  refresh();
  return root;
}
// %%
md`## The seed

The Cloudflare API answers a browser's preflight with 400 and no CORS headers, so a page cannot call it. The seed is a Worker the installer pastes into the Cloudflare dashboard by hand: it forwards \`/client/v4/…\` to \`api.cloudflare.com\` and adds CORS headers. It holds no credential; the page sends the token with each call. While it exists anyone who knows its URL can use it as a relay to the Cloudflare API, with their own token, so an install deletes it last.`
// %%
seedSource = `// Cloud Brain seed: a CORS proxy to the Cloudflare API, in your own account. Holds no credential.
const cors = { "access-control-allow-origin": "*", "access-control-allow-headers": "authorization, content-type", "access-control-allow-methods": "GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE", "access-control-max-age": "86400" };
export default {
  async fetch(request) {
    if (request.method === "OPTIONS") return new Response(null, { headers: cors });
    const url = new URL(request.url);
    if (!url.pathname.startsWith("/client/v4/")) return new Response("Cloud Brain seed", { headers: cors });
    const headers = new Headers();
    for (const name of ["authorization", "content-type"]) if (request.headers.has(name)) headers.set(name, request.headers.get(name));
    const upstream = await fetch("https://api.cloudflare.com" + url.pathname + url.search, { method: request.method, headers, body: ["GET", "HEAD"].includes(request.method) ? undefined : request.body });
    const out = new Headers(upstream.headers);
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(cors)) out.set(k, v);
    return new Response(upstream.body, { status: upstream.status, headers: out });
  },
};
`
// %%
md`## The example service

\`fx_service\` is a Hono app that reads a secret, writes a row, appends to the inbox, calls out, and uses a helper cell, a constant and a function imported from another module. The tests below run it three ways.`
// %%
fx_service = cloudflare.Service("fixture", fx_app, {
  paths: [{ path: "/hooks/whatsapp", who: "anyone" }],
  methods: { "com.lopecode.brain.fixture.math": { type: "query" } }
})
// %%
fx_emitted = (await fx_service.emit()).parts.map((p) => ({ path: p.path, bytes: p.text.length }))
// %%
md`## API`
// %%
platformTag = Symbol.for("cloudflare-iac.platform")
// %%
secretName = /^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*$/
// %%
// The notebook side of the platform cells. In memory until a Brain is configured with backend.use().
backend = {
  const store = { secrets: new Map(), rows: new Map(), blobs: new Map(), inbox: [], calls: [] };
  return {
    store,
    secret: async (name) => store.secrets.get(name),
    rowsGet: async (key) => (store.rows.has(key) ? store.rows.get(key) : null),
    rowsPut: async (key, value) => (store.rows.set(key, value), true),
    rowsDelete: async (key) => store.rows.delete(key),
    rowsList: async (prefix = "") =>
      [...store.rows].filter(([k]) => k.startsWith(prefix)).map(([key, value]) => ({ key, value })),
    xrpc: async (kind, nsid, data) => {
      if (nsid === "com.lopecode.brain.inbox.append") {
        const seen = store.inbox.find((e) => e.key === data.key);
        if (seen) return { id: seen.id, duplicate: true };
        const entry = { id: store.inbox.length + 1, ...data };
        store.inbox.push(entry);
        return { id: entry.id };
      }
      store.calls.push({ kind, nsid, data });
      throw new Error("no Brain configured for " + nsid);
    },
    rowsPutIfAbsent: async (key, value) => (store.rows.has(key) ? false : (store.rows.set(key, value), true)),
    rowsIncrement: async (key) => (store.rows.set(key, (store.rows.get(key) || 0) + 1), store.rows.get(key)),
    rowsAppend: async (key, item, max = 1000) => (store.rows.set(key, [...(store.rows.get(key) || []), item].slice(-max)), store.rows.get(key).length),
    rowsDeletePrefix: async (prefix) => [...store.rows.keys()].filter((k) => k.startsWith(prefix)).map((k) => store.rows.delete(k)).length,
    sqlRun: async () => {
      throw new Error("sql runs in a Worker, or under simulate with a sql function");
    },
    databaseRun: async () => {
      throw new Error("database runs in brain-db, or under simulate with a sql function");
    },
    blobGet: async (key) => store.blobs.get(key) ?? null,
    blobPut: async (key, bytes) => (store.blobs.set(key, bytes), true),
    blobHas: async (key) => store.blobs.has(key),
    blobDelete: async (key) => store.blobs.delete(key),
    xrpcFetch: async (nsid) => {
      throw new Error("no Brain configured for " + nsid);
    },
    config: {},
    forward: async (role, request) => {
      throw new Error("no Brain configured: cannot reach " + role);
    },
    deploy: null,
    use(impl) {
      Object.assign(this, impl);
      return this;
    }
  };
}
// %%
secrets = new Proxy(
  {},
  {
    get: (_, k) =>
      k === platformTag
        ? "secrets"
        : typeof k === "string" && secretName.test(k)
        ? backend.secret(k)
        : undefined
  }
)
// %%
rows = ({
  [platformTag]: "rows",
  get: (key) => backend.rowsGet(key),
  put: (key, value) => backend.rowsPut(key, value),
  delete: (key) => backend.rowsDelete(key),
  list: (prefix) => backend.rowsList(prefix),
  putIfAbsent: (key, value) => backend.rowsPutIfAbsent(key, value),
  increment: (key) => backend.rowsIncrement(key),
  // One step each, so two callers cannot interleave: push onto the list at key (keeping the last max), and delete every key under a prefix.
  append: (key, item, max) => backend.rowsAppend(key, item, max),
  deletePrefix: (prefix) => backend.rowsDeletePrefix(prefix)
})
// %%
// SQL tables in the Brain's one table database. A Worker has no binding to it: each call is db.sql on brain-db, which
// decides per table from the statement's plan (the rules are in @tomlarkworthy/brain-db). A Worker owns the tables
// named with its prefix, brain-x-metrics the tables metrics_*. exec answers { rows, changes }; batch takes
// [[statement, ...args], …] and runs them as one transaction. One statement each, with no ";".
sql = ({
  [platformTag]: "sql",
  exec: (statement, ...args) => backend.sqlRun([[statement, ...args]]).then((r) => r[0]),
  batch: (list) => backend.sqlRun(list)
})
// %%
// A D1 database of the Worker's own, with no check. The deployer gives one to brain-db (the table database) and one to
// brain-x-knowledge, and to no other Worker.
database = ({
  [platformTag]: "database",
  batch: (list) => backend.databaseRun(list)
})
// %%
blobs = ({
  [platformTag]: "blobs",
  get: (key) => backend.blobGet(key),
  put: (key, bytes) => backend.blobPut(key, bytes),
  has: (key) => backend.blobHas(key),
  delete: (key) => backend.blobDelete(key)
})
// %%
// rows, read through a few seconds of this instance's memory: for what is read on every call and written rarely
// (a route table, access rules). A write goes to rows and drops this instance's copy; another instance sees it
// within ttl. Not for a read that is followed by a write of the same key: use rows.
settings = {
  const held = new Map();
  const read = async (k, load) => {
    const h = held.get(k);
    if (h && Date.now() - h.at < api.ttl) return h.value;
    const value = await load();
    held.set(k, { at: Date.now(), value });
    return value;
  };
  const api = {
    ttl: 5000,
    get: (key) => read("get " + key, () => rows.get(key)),
    list: (prefix = "") => read("list " + prefix, () => rows.list(prefix)),
    put: async (key, value) => {
      const done = await rows.put(key, value);
      held.clear();
      return done;
    },
    delete: async (key) => {
      const done = await rows.delete(key);
      held.clear();
      return done;
    },
    clear: () => held.clear()
  };
  return api;
}
// %%
config = new Proxy({}, { get: (_, k) => (k === platformTag ? "config" : typeof k === "string" ? backend.config[k] : undefined) })
// %%
core = ({ [platformTag]: "core", fetch: (request) => backend.forward("core", request) })
// %%
deployer = ({ [platformTag]: "deployer", fetch: (request) => backend.forward("deployer", request) })
// %%
// The name of the deployer cell until 2026-10-08. A module that imports guard gets the same cell.
guard = deployer
// %%
assets = ({ [platformTag]: "assets", fetch: (request) => backend.forward("assets", request) })
// %%
// Cloudflare Browser Run: remote browsers, billed by the browser-hour. fetch(path, init) is the binding's own
// HTTP form ("/v1/devtools/browser", "/v1/sessions"); an Upgrade request answers with a WebSocket that carries CDP.
browser = ({ [platformTag]: "browser", fetch: (path, init = {}) => backend.forward("browser", new Request("https://browser.internal" + path, init)) })
// %%
// log({ at: "poll.skipped", reason }) writes one line to Workers Logs: the object, with this Worker's name and the
// ray of the call being answered. Cloudflare keeps each field, 7 days. Write names and decisions; never a secret,
// a header or a body.
log = Object.assign((entry) => console.log({ ...entry, worker: "notebook", ray: "" }), { [platformTag]: "log" })
// %%
// The Cloudflare API for this account, as the token the deployer minted for this Worker allows. A service has it
// only when it declares its permission groups: cloudflare.Service(name, fn, { cloudflare: ["Workers Observability Read"] }).
// fetch(path, init): path is what follows /accounts/ACCOUNT. The answer is Cloudflare's own Response.
cloudflareApi = ({ [platformTag]: "cloudflareApi", fetch: (path, init = {}) => backend.forward("cloudflareApi", new Request("https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts/ACCOUNT" + path, init)) })
// %%
workers = ({
  [platformTag]: "workers",
  has: (name) => false,
  fetch: (name, request) => backend.forward(name, request)
})
// %%
inbox = ({
  [platformTag]: "inbox",
  append: (entry) => backend.xrpc("procedure", "com.lopecode.brain.inbox.append", entry)
})
// %%
xrpc = ({
  [platformTag]: "xrpc",
  query: (nsid, params) => backend.xrpc("query", nsid, params),
  procedure: (nsid, input) => backend.xrpc("procedure", nsid, input),
  // The same call with the body and the answer as they are: bytes in, a Response out, no error thrown for a status.
  fetch: (nsid, options) => backend.xrpcFetch(nsid, options)
})
// %%
libraries = new Map()
// %%
// A self-contained ES module carried to the Worker as its own file. `source` is text or a FileAttachment.
library = async (name, source) => {
  const text = typeof source === "string" ? source : await source.text();
  const ns = await import(URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([text], { type: "text/javascript" })));
  libraries.set(ns, { name, text });
  return ns;
}
// %%
cloudflare = {
  // A service: a name, a function and a manifest. It is deployed as one Cloudflare Worker, inside the wrapper.
  const Service = (name, fn, options = {}) => {
    const service = {
      name,
      fn,
      options,
      fetch: async (request, { caller = "owner" } = {}) => {
        const req = request instanceof Request ? request : new Request(request);
        return typeof fn?.fetch === "function" ? fn.fetch(req, {}, {}) : fn(req, { caller, info: {} });
      },
      emit: () => emit(service),
      deploy: async (reason, was) => {
        if (!backend.deploy) throw new Error("no Brain configured: deploy() needs a deployer");
        return backend.deploy(await emit(service), reason, was);
      }
    };
    return service;
  };
  // Worker: the name of Service until 2026-10-07. The same function.
  return { [platformTag]: "cloudflare", Service, Worker: Service };
}
// %%
// Everything below the notebook cells in an emitted module. Runs on the Worker and under simulate().
workerRuntime = (AsyncLocalStorage = null) => {
  let ENV = null;
  const tag = Symbol.for("cloudflare-iac.platform");
  const info = () => (ENV && ENV.BRAIN_INFO) || {};
  const json = (value, status = 200) => Response.json(value, { status });
  // Origin. With a call the core forwards comes a reference to who started the chain (x-brain-context, see Origin in
  // brain-core). It is held for the time this Worker answers that call, and goes back with each call made to the core
  // in that time: secrets, sql, inbox, xrpc. Work the Worker starts itself (a schedule) holds none.
  // On Cloudflare the holder is AsyncLocalStorage (flag nodejs_als), so two calls answered at once do not see each
  // other's. A browser has none: under simulate() one call at a time is held, and while two overlap none is passed on.
  const held = AsyncLocalStorage ? new AsyncLocalStorage() : null;
  let one = null, open = 0, mixed = false;
  const during = async (store, fn) => {
    if (held) return held.run(store, fn);
    if (!(ENV && ENV.__sim)) return fn();
    if (open++ > 0) mixed = true;
    else one = store;
    try {
      return await fn();
    } finally {
      if (--open === 0) (one = null), (mixed = false);
    }
  };
  const chain = () => (held ? held.getStore() : ENV && ENV.__sim && !mixed ? one : null) || null;
  const chained = (headers) => {
    const now = chain();
    const out = now && now.context && !("x-brain-context" in headers) ? { ...headers, "x-brain-context": now.context } : headers;
    return now && now.ray ? { ...out, "x-brain-ray": now.ray } : out;
  };
  const core = async (kind, nsid, data) => {
    if (!ENV.CORE) throw new Error("no core binding for " + nsid);
    const query = kind === "query";
    const url = "https://core.internal/xrpc/" + nsid + (query && data ? "?" + new URLSearchParams(data) : "");
    const headers = { ...chained({ "content-type": "application/json" }), "x-brain-key": ENV.BRAIN_KEY || "" };
    const r = await ENV.CORE.fetch(
      new Request(url, query ? { headers } : { method: "POST", headers, body: JSON.stringify(data ?? {}) })
    );
    const body = await r.json().catch(() => null);
    if (!r.ok) throw Object.assign(new Error(body?.message || nsid + " " + r.status), { error: body?.error, status: r.status });
    return body;
  };
  const coreFetch = (nsid, { method = "GET", params = null, body = null, headers = {} } = {}) => {
    if (!ENV.CORE) throw new Error("no core binding for " + nsid);
    const url = "https://core.internal/xrpc/" + nsid + (params ? "?" + new URLSearchParams(params) : "");
    return ENV.CORE.fetch(new Request(url, { method, headers: { ...chained(headers), "x-brain-key": ENV.BRAIN_KEY || "" }, body: method === "GET" ? undefined : body }));
  };
  // The bucket is one per Brain. A recipe's keys are kept under its own name, so two recipes cannot read each other's.
  const blobKey = (key) => (ENV.BRAIN_INFO && ENV.BRAIN_INFO.role === "recipe" ? ENV.BRAIN_INFO.name + "/" : "") + key;
  // rows on D1: one database per Brain, one table, a Worker's rows under its own name. Each op is one statement,
  // so two callers cannot interleave inside one. Nothing is billed while no statement runs.
  let sqlReady = null;
  const sqlCall = async (op, key, value) => {
    const t = info().name || "";
    if (ENV.SQL.sim) return ENV.SQL.sim(op, key, value);
    await (sqlReady ??= ENV.SQL.prepare("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS kv (t TEXT NOT NULL, k TEXT NOT NULL, v TEXT NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (t, k)) WITHOUT ROWID")
      .run()
      .catch((e) => {
        sqlReady = null;
        throw e;
      }));
    const q = (sql, ...args) => ENV.SQL.prepare(sql).bind(t, ...args);
    // Every key that starts with the prefix: from it, up to it followed by the last code point.
    const hi = (prefix) => prefix + "\u{10FFFF}";
    if (op === "get") {
      const r = await q("SELECT v FROM kv WHERE t = ?1 AND k = ?2", key).first();
      return r ? JSON.parse(r.v) : null;
    }
    if (op === "put") return await q("INSERT INTO kv (t, k, v) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3) ON CONFLICT (t, k) DO UPDATE SET v = excluded.v", key, JSON.stringify(value)).run(), true;
    if (op === "delete") return (await q("DELETE FROM kv WHERE t = ?1 AND k = ?2", key).run()).meta.changes > 0;
    if (op === "list") {
      const r = await q("SELECT k, v FROM kv WHERE t = ?1 AND k >= ?2 AND k < ?3 ORDER BY k", key || "", hi(key || "")).all();
      return r.results.map((x) => ({ key: x.k, value: JSON.parse(x.v) }));
    }
    if (op === "putIfAbsent") return (await q("INSERT OR IGNORE INTO kv (t, k, v) VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3)", key, JSON.stringify(value)).run()).meta.changes > 0;
    if (op === "increment")
      return Number((await q("INSERT INTO kv (t, k, v) VALUES (?1, ?2, '1') ON CONFLICT (t, k) DO UPDATE SET v = CAST(CAST(v AS INTEGER) + 1 AS TEXT) RETURNING v", key).first()).v);
    if (op === "append") {
      const item = JSON.stringify(value.item), max = value.max || 1000;
      let n = (await q("INSERT INTO kv (t, k, v) VALUES (?1, ?2, json_array(json(?3))) ON CONFLICT (t, k) DO UPDATE SET v = json_insert(v, '$[#]', json(?3)) RETURNING json_array_length(v) AS n", key, item).first()).n;
      for (; n > max; n--) await q("UPDATE kv SET v = json_remove(v, '$[0]') WHERE t = ?1 AND k = ?2", key).run();
      return n;
    }
    if (op === "deletePrefix") return (await q("DELETE FROM kv WHERE t = ?1 AND k >= ?2 AND k < ?3", key || "", hi(key || "")).run()).meta.changes;
    throw new Error("rows: no op " + op);
  };
  // sql: db.sql on brain-db, through the core. database: the D1 binding, which only brain-db has. A batch is one transaction.
  const sqlRun = async (list) => (await core("procedure", "com.lopecode.brain.db.sql", { statements: list })).results;
  const databaseRun = async (list) => {
    if (!ENV.DB) throw new Error("this Worker is not bound to the table database");
    if (ENV.DB.sim) {
      const out = [];
      for (const [statement, ...args] of list) out.push(await ENV.DB.sim(statement, args));
      return out;
    }
    const shape = (r) => ({ rows: r.results || [], changes: (r.meta && r.meta.changes) || 0 });
    const made = list.map(([statement, ...args]) => ENV.DB.prepare(statement).bind(...args));
    return (await ENV.DB.batch(made)).map(shape);
  };
  // The deployer keeps its rows in a Durable Object, which also carries the alarm that ticks it.
  const rowsCall = async (op, key, value) => {
    if (ENV.SQL) return sqlCall(op, key, value);
    const stub = ENV.ROWS.get(ENV.ROWS.idFromName("rows"));
    const r = await stub.fetch(new Request("https://rows.internal/", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify({ op, key, value }) }));
    return (await r.json()).value;
  };
  // A Worker proves which Worker it is to the core and the deployer with the key the deployer gave it at deploy.
  const keyedRequest = (request) => {
    const headers = new Headers(request.headers);
    headers.set("x-brain-key", ENV.BRAIN_KEY || "");
    return new Request(request, { headers });
  };
  // The text of every prose cell (a cell that returns one md`…`) in a module's source, in order, with the escapes of a
  // template literal undone. A method table in a later cell is part of the reference too.
  const referenceOf = (text) =>
    [...text.matchAll(/\{\s*return \(\s*md`((?:[^`\\]|\\[\s\S])*)`/g)].map((m) => m[1].replace(/\\([`\\$])/g, "$1")).join("\n\n");
  const bindingName = (name) => "X_" + name.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9]/g, "_");
  const secretsHeld = new Map();
  // Settings the owner stored for this Worker (config.set, rows of the core). The core sends them with each call it
  // forwards, so a change is read with no deploy and no call of this Worker's own. A key the deployer bound in
  // BRAIN_CONFIG (owner, host, base…) is never replaced. The kernel, the core and the deployer take none.
  let stored = null, storedAt = 0;
  const configRead = new Set();
  const takesSettings = () => !["kernel", "core", "deployer", "guard"].includes(info().role);
  const settingsFrom = (header) => {
    try {
      const v = JSON.parse(decodeURIComponent(header));
      return v && typeof v === "object" && !Array.isArray(v) ? v : {};
    } catch {
      return {};
    }
  };
  // A Worker run by its clock has no call to read them from. It reads the core's row itself: one statement, when
  // it has no copy or the copy is 5 minutes old. A Worker with no database binding (a member's) reads none.
  const settingsForTick = async () => {
    if (!takesSettings() || !ENV.SQL || Date.now() - storedAt < 300000) return;
    try {
      const key = "config/" + (info().name || "");
      // Under simulate() the test's rows stand in for the core's.
      const row = ENV.SQL.sim ? await ENV.SQL.sim("get", key) : await ENV.SQL.prepare("SELECT v FROM kv WHERE t = ?1 AND k = ?2").bind("brain-core", key).first().then((r) => (r ? JSON.parse(r.v) : null));
      stored = row && typeof row === "object" ? row : {};
      storedAt = Date.now();
    } catch {}
  };
  const configOf = (k) => {
    const bound = ENV.BRAIN_CONFIG || {};
    return Object.hasOwn(bound, k) ? { value: bound[k], from: "deploy" } : stored && Object.hasOwn(stored, k) ? { value: stored[k], from: "stored" } : { value: undefined, from: "unset" };
  };
  const platform = {
    cloudflare: ((Service) => ({ [tag]: "cloudflare", Service, Worker: Service }))((name, fn, options = {}) => ({ name, fn, options })),
    // A key the platform bound (this Worker's own key, the kernel's cookie key, the deployer's token) is read from the
    // binding. Any other secret is asked of secret.get as this Worker, which answers by the secret's rule, and is
    // held for 5 s: a new value or a changed rule is followed without a deploy. Undefined when unset or refused.
    // A secret's rule can read the origin, so a value is held for one origin and not handed to another.
